[38;5;231m⏺[39m Fourth question — module format, which also decides how (and whether) this gets tested:

  [1mThe fork:[0m [38;5;153mindex.html[39m loads [38;5;153mapp.js[39m as a plain classic script with globals, while [38;5;153msrc/[39m is CommonJS for Node. A shared
  validator has to pick a lane, and that pick decides whether you can unit-test it without a browser.

  - [1mES modules[0m ([38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, [38;5;153mimport[39m) — one format for browser and modern Node, directly unit-testable,
    standard. Cost: [38;5;153mindex.html[39m must be served over HTTP; opening the file directly via [38;5;153mfile://[39m stops working.
  - [1mClassic script + global[0m ([38;5;153mwindow.Validation = ...[39m, second [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag) — zero change to how you run the app, still
    works on [38;5;153mfile://[39m. Cost: not importable by a Node test runner without a shim, so tests need jsdom or a hack.
  - [1mCommonJS in [38;5;153msrc/[39m + a build step[0m — matches the existing [38;5;153msrc/[39m island and tests cleanly. Cost: introduces a bundler to
    a project that currently has no dependencies at all. I'd avoid this.

  [1mMy recommendation: ES modules.[0m It's the only option that makes the validator testable with no dependencies, and the
  [38;5;153mfile://[39m cost is one [38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m away. If you actually open [38;5;153mindex.html[39m by double-clicking it, say so and
  I'll take the global.
